## 3. Hard Surface Modeling and Maya Workflows

Hard surface modeling underpins environment art, weapons, vehicles, and props. **N-gon workflows have replaced quad obsession** for non-deforming game assets, as documented extensively in Polycount discussions.

### Industry-standard courses

**Gnomon Workshop’s “Hard Surface Modeling 1 & 2”** (10 weeks, $2,061) cover fundamentals through complex vehicles, robots, and weapons with explicit focus on poly count, surface quality, and topology. These courses set the industry benchmark for film and AAA games.

**FlippedNormals’ “Hard Surface Modeling for Games”** (13 hours, real-time Maya hovercraft) specifically targets AAA production with UV workflow integration. Their “Hard Surface Modeling Foundation” covers principles across two complex projects.

**Arrimus 3D** (YouTube, 600+ free tutorials) provides the best free hard surface education. The channel demonstrates Maya techniques with emphasis on clean topology and efficient workflows. The **Udemy courses on Plasticity** cover the emerging CAD-style modeling approach gaining traction.

**CGMA’s “Hard Surface Modeling for Characters”** (6+ weeks) specifically addresses blockout, range of motion testing, and retopology for game meshes—animation-aware hard surface work often neglected in other courses.

**3Dniator’s Udemy courses** by Shoaib Mohammed (credits on Forza, GTA Trilogy) offer “3D Modeling for Games: Create AAA Assets in Maya & Substance” with direct AAA pipeline experience.

### The essential Japanese reference

**“Maya実践ハードサーフェスモデリング”** (Maya Practical Hard Surface Modeling) by Eiji Kitada represents the definitive Japanese industry reference. Published by Born Digital (¥5,060, 404 pages, B5 full color), it covers production workflow for film, commercials, and game cinematics including asset management, linear workflow, UDIM UV layout, and texture painting. Concept art contributors include Koji Tajima and Kenichiro Tomiyasu. This book is **essential for understanding Japanese studio approaches**.

**ModIt**, a hard surface modeling script for Maya available at WZX STORE, is popular among Japanese modelers with detailed 32-minute tutorials available.

FromSoftware’s modeling pipeline involves extensive outsourcing to **Lakshya Digital** (character outfits, weapons, props for Elden Ring) and **Passion Republic** (character outfits and creatures). Tools include Maya, ZBrush, and Substance Painter with thorough 2D concept to final 3D model review processes.

### Chinese resources

Bilibili’s **“Maya建模之第一季终极硬表面布线教程”** (71,000+ views) provides complete beginner-to-advanced edge flow instruction. **“【免费教程】Maya硬表面机械建模高级流程教学”** demonstrates electromagnetic cannon modeling with efficiency tips and modern plugins.

**Shen Jia** (PaperGames, formerly Archosaur Games) published a notable **stylized Elden Ring Ghibli-style recreation** on 80.lv documenting his Maya → Substance 3D Designer/Sampler → Unreal Engine workflow.

miHoYo’s topology approach uses **unique diamond-pattern triangulation** for better deformation with grid-like vertex placement for optimal skinning—discussed in Polycount threads with official models available on the Chinese platform “aplaybox.”

### GDC talks on environment and prop production

**“The Material Art of The Last of Us Part I”** (GDC 2023) by Jonathan Benainous and Jared Sobotta details Naughty Dog’s **100+ procedural Substance 3D Designer materials** for Boston Capitol Building architectural materials and Tommy’s Dam organic materials.

**“Driving Innovation: A New Vehicle Pipeline for The Last of Us: Part II”** (GDC 2021) by Matthew Trevelyan Johns covers the new system for highly detailed vehicles—essential viewing for prop artists.

**“The Environment Art Technology and Workflows of Alan Wake 2”** (GDC 2024) by Remedy’s Miro Vesterinen and Benjamin Lindquist covers GPU-based skinning for dense vegetation, OpenUSD integration for world building, and data-oriented ECS frameworks.

**“God of War Ragnarök Rendering”** (GDC 2023) addresses cross-generation PS4/PS5 development with raytraced cubemaps and contact hardening shadows. Environment artist Jon Arellano’s ArtStation breakdowns of kit assets demonstrate practical modular approaches.

### Topology knowledge

**Polycount Wiki** (wiki.polycount.com/wiki/Topology) remains the definitive free topology reference covering body, face, limb, and shoulder topology with pole placement and T-vertex guidance. The forum discussion **“Topology standards for hard surface modeling?”** explicitly addresses why **N-gon workflows are standard for hard surface game assets**—quad obsession is “outdated myth” for non-deforming meshes.

