## 4. Character Animation for Games

Game animation diverged from film animation as **Motion Matching technology** replaced traditional state machines at elite studios. Understanding both traditional principles and modern systems is now essential.

### Expert-level courses

**iAnimate** (ianimate.net) offers the **best dedicated game animation track**—four workshops of 11 weeks each covering game-specific production pipelines. Founded by Jason Ryan (Supervising Animator at DreamWorks), instructors come from Pixar, DreamWorks, Disney, and Bungie. The Motion Capture workshop covers industry integration directly.

**Animation Mentor**, the original online animation school (founded 2005), now offers a **dedicated Game Animation Track** integrating Maya with Unreal Engine. “Game Animation: Fundamentals” covers idle poses and engine basics; “Game Animation: Mechanics & Interactive Gameplay” addresses character movesets and environmental interactions. Mentors include specialists from Blizzard and Riot Games.

**AnimSchool** (founded by former Blue Sky Studios animator David Gallagher) provides more affordable alternatives with strong VR and game coverage. 30+ instructors from Disney, Pixar, DreamWorks, and Sony teach the 3D Animation and Character programs.

### The definitive GDC talk on locomotion

**“Motion Matching in The Last of Us Part II”** (GDC 2021) by Naughty Dog’s Michal Mach (Head of Technical/NPC Animation) and John Bellomy represents **essential viewing for modern game animators**. The presentation details how Motion Matching replaced traditional state machines with frame-by-frame pose selection from hundreds of animation “buckets.” This technology powers locomotion, traversal, melee, and cover systems in TLOU2 and defines the industry direction.

**“Filling Marston’s Boots: Player Locomotion in Red Dead Redemption 2”** (GDC 2020) by Rockstar’s Mike Jones examines their controversial decision to prioritize realistic animation over responsive controls—**5-10 frame delay for state switching** traded control for immersion.

**“Unreal Engine 5.4: Animation Deep Dive”** (GDC 2024) covers Epic’s official Motion Matching implementation for LEGO Fortnite and Fortnite Battle Royale locomotion. **Tencent’s “MotorNerve”** GDC presentation demonstrates Motion Matching combined with Learned Motion Matching and Motion In-Betweening algorithms.

Santa Monica Studio’s **“Animation Bootcamp: God of War: Breathing New Life into a Hardened Spartan”** (GDC 2019) by Bruno Velazquez explains reinventing Kratos for the 2018 reboot with the one continuous camera shot requirement shaping animation design. The GDC 2023 Ragnarök presentations cover joint-based rigging for volume preservation and combat accessibility innovations.

### Japanese animation philosophy

**FromSoftware’s “animation priority and timing”** principle defines their combat feel. Deliberate animations prioritize weight and commitment over responsiveness through delayed telegraphed attacks, input buffering preventing animation interruption, I-frame based survival mechanics, and distinct recovery animations creating punish windows. This philosophy evolved from Demon’s Souls through Elden Ring, with Sekiro introducing high-tempo parry-focused mechanics.

**PlatinumGames** (founded by Shinji Mikami, Atsushi Inaba, and Hideki Kamiya from Capcom/Clover Studio) represents the opposite philosophy—fast-paced, responsive, exaggerated action in Bayonetta, Vanquish, and NieR: Automata. Studying both approaches reveals the full spectrum of Japanese action game animation.

**Capcom’s animation heritage** runs from Street Fighter Alpha’s cartoon-style graphics through Darkstalkers’ expressive 2D animation to the modern RE Engine powering Resident Evil, Monster Hunter, and Devil May Cry. Their philosophy balances Japanese sensibilities with worldwide audience development.

Anime influence manifests in expressive characters, dramatic sequences, and non-photorealistic rendering—visible in Persona, Genshin Impact, and Fire Emblem. Studio Ghibli’s collaboration on Ni No Kuni demonstrates the high end of this aesthetic applied to games.

### Chinese resources

**Bilibili** serves as the primary platform for Chinese game animation content. Search “游戏动画” (game animation) or “角色动画” (character animation) for tutorials. miHoYo streams version updates directly on Bilibili, and their **GDC 2021 presentation** revealed Genshin Impact’s dual rendering pipeline (deferred for scenes, forward for NPR characters) and 17 new characters per year design process.

### The essential books

**“The Animator’s Survival Kit”** by Richard Williams remains **the foundational text for all animators**. Williams directed animation on “Who Framed Roger Rabbit,” won three Academy Awards, and was voted “Animator’s Animator” in 1990. The subtitle explicitly includes games: “A Manual of Methods, Principles and Formulas for Classical, Computer, Games, Stop Motion and Internet Animators.” A 16-DVD animated edition and iBook version extend the book’s lessons.

**“Game Anim: Video Game Animation Explained”** by Jonathan Cooper (2nd Edition) is **the game-specific companion**. Cooper’s credits include Assassin’s Creed, Mass Effect, Uncharted, and The Last of Us; he currently works at Apple on visionOS animation. The second edition adds a new chapter on 2D/Pixel Art Animation and enhanced motion capture coverage including Motion Matching. The free AZRI Maya rig and companion website (gameanim.com/book) provide practical resources.

### Unreal Engine animation resources

**Motion Matching documentation** at dev.epicgames.com covers the Pose Search plugin providing query-based animation pose selection without transition logic. The **Game Animation Sample Project** (free from Unreal Marketplace) includes **500+ free locomotion animations** with complete Motion Matching setup—an invaluable learning resource.

State Machines, Blend Spaces (1D/2D for locomotion), and Aim Offsets documentation provides the traditional foundation that Motion Matching builds upon.

