## Cross-cutting resources and recommendations

### Elite studio post-mortems worth studying

Naughty Dog, Santa Monica Studio, and Guerrilla Games publish the most detailed technical art documentation through GDC Vault. FromSoftware’s approaches are reconstructed through outsource studio portfolios (Lakshya Digital, Passion Republic) and artist testimonials rather than official presentations.

### Books every technical artist should own

- **“Anatomy for Sculptors”** by Uldis Zarins (character production foundation)
- **“The Animator’s Survival Kit”** by Richard Williams (animation principles)
- **“Game Anim”** by Jonathan Cooper (game-specific animation)
- **“Houdini Foundations”** by Robert Magee (free PDF, procedural foundation)
- **“Maya実践ハードサーフェスモデリング”** by Eiji Kitada (Japanese hard surface reference)

### Platform access priorities

**GDC Vault** subscription provides access to the most valuable industry documentation. **Gnomon Workshop** ($490/year) covers all four domains at the highest level. **SideFX Learn** and **Unreal Engine documentation** provide essential free foundations for procedural and real-time work.

### Notable instructors to follow

- **Steven Knipping** (Applied Houdini) - ILM production workflows
- **Ryan Kingslien** - Anatomy and sculpting methodology
- **Jonathan Cooper** (@GameAnim) - Game animation principles
- **Bruno Velazquez** - Santa Monica animation direction
- **Madeleine Scott-Spencer** - Gnomon ZBrush instruction

This guide prioritizes resources used by elite studios for production work. The combination of Western structured education, Japanese philosophical approaches, and Chinese community translations provides the most comprehensive preparation for professional technical art careers.